<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Farabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–950</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Nasr al-Farabi, Alpharabius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Farabi was a philosopher of the Islamic world, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and spent his last years at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, where he died around 950. Called the 'Second Teacher' after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, he wrote commentaries on Greek logic, a treatise on the virtuous city and influential works on music. He shaped the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-farabi.jpg" alt="Al-Farabi" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr.Nostalgic · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpharabius_in_Liber_Chronicarum_1493_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of al-Farabi's life. The accounts of his origins vary and seem to rest on hearsay or guesses, and Arabic biographers of the 12th and 13th centuries had few facts and used invented stories. His full name was Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Farabi, sometimes with the family name al-Tarkhani. His birthplace could have been any of several places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, then called Khurasan, since farab is a Persian term for land irrigated by springs or a nearby river. Candidates include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Farab</a> (Otrar) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, a village near Türkmenabat and Faryab in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><p>From incidental accounts it is known that he spent most of his scholarly life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> with Syriac Christian scholars, among them Yuhanna ibn Haylan, Yahya ibn Adi and Abu Ishaq Ibrahim al-Baghdadi. He later spent time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> before returning to Damascus, where he died around the middle of the 10th century. His religious affiliation is disputed, and some scholars place him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and others as Shia or influenced by Shia thought.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Al-Farabi is credited with over one hundred works, among them prolegomena to philosophy and commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, including the Nicomachean Ethics. His main influence was the Aristotelian tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, with elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a> and of Ptolemy's planetary model. In logic he was mainly an Aristotelian but discussed future contingents, the relation of logic to grammar and Stoic forms of inference, and he introduced the concept of the poetic syllogism in a commentary on Aristotle's Poetics. He also wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>, in The Necessity of the Art of the Elixir, and was a practical musician and music theorist.</p><p>In metaphysics he held that the subject was being as such, related to God only as a principle of absolute being, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> remarked that he understood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Metaphysics only after reading al-Farabi's prolegomenon. His cosmology had the First Cause, by contemplating itself, give rise to a cascade of ten intellects, each bringing a celestial sphere into being, beneath which lies the material world. The process was necessary rather than willed, a view that al-Ghazzali later criticised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Later writers called al-Farabi the Second Master, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> the first. He is credited as the first Muslim to present philosophy as a coherent system, and he is called the founder of Islamic political philosophy. Among those he influenced are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus">Albertus Magnus</a>. He helped to pass on Aristotle's thought to the Christian West, and he is the only philosopher named in Maimonides's Treatise on Logic.</p><p>In Kazakhstan a large university, KazNU, bears his name, and monuments to him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Alma-Ata</a>, Shymkent and Turkestan. An anniversary of his birth was marked in 1975, and the asteroid 7057 Al-Fārābī is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Farabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His court gathered the poet al-Mutanabbi, his cousin the poet Abu Firas, and the philosopher <b>al-Farabi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otrar">Otrar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.85,68.3&amp;year=922" aria-label="Otrar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6201 642 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Farabi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6651 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6651 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.85,68.3&amp;year=922">What was here in 922 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
